Every pointer comparison is signed
==================================

	p >= q		/* both struct store * */

compiles to a signed comparison.  Two pointers either side of 0x8000
therefore compare backwards: an address of 0x8008 is "less than" one of
0x7ff6, because as signed sixteen bit numbers they are -32760 and
+32758.

This has been true of every pointer comparison the compiler has ever
emitted.  It cannot show up until a program holds live data on both
sides of 0x8000 and compares across the boundary, which on a 64K
machine means a heap that has grown past 32K.


Reproducing it
--------------

	struct store { struct store *ptr; char flag; };

	a(p, q)
	struct store *p, *q;
	{
		return p >= q;
	}

	ccc -m micronix -s cmp.c

gives

		ld	l,(iy+6)	; q
		ld	h,(iy+7)
		ld	e,(iy+4)	; p
		ld	d,(iy+5)
		ex	de,hl
		or	a
		sbc	hl,de
		ld	a,h
		jp	po,$+5		; overflow?  then the sign bit
		xor	80h		; of the difference is inverted
		or	a
		ld	a,0
		jp	m,$+4
		inc	a

"sbc hl,de / ld a,h / jp po / xor 80h / or a / jp m" is the signed
comparison idiom: subtract, and if the subtraction overflowed, correct
the sign bit before testing it.  An unsigned comparison is just the
subtract and a test of carry - "sbc hl,de / jr c" - and that is what
this should have been.


Where it comes from
-------------------

Not from a cast, and not from operand order.  Pointers do not have a
type by the time the code generator sees them.  pass2's whole type
vocabulary is

	#define	T_BYTE   'b'	#define	T_UBYTE  'B'
	#define	T_SHORT  's'	#define	T_USHORT 'S'
	#define	T_LONG   'l'	#define	T_ULONG  'L'
	#define	T_VOID   'v'

	#define	ISSIGNED(w)  ((w) == 'b' || (w) == 's' || (w) == 'l')

There is no pointer in that list.  An address arrives as a sixteen bit
integer, the default sixteen bit integer is short, and short is signed
- so ISSIGNED says yes and the relop rules emit the signed sequence.
The AST dumps say the same thing out loud once you know to look:

	(DEREF:short (LOCALVAR:short IY+8))
	(HL:short)

Everything is :short.  Pointers never acquired signedness; they lost
their type.

pass1 knows perfectly well that these are pointers.  The AST it hands
to pass2 should say so, by marking a pointer-valued expression
unsigned - 'S' rather than 's'.  ISSIGNED then does the right thing for
free, and every rule that consults it follows, without pass2 needing to
learn about pointers at all.


What it cost
------------

wsld could not link peep on the machine: "out of memory" with 38K of
heap consumed and almost no work done.

malloc guards its block search against address wraparound:

	if (q >= (struct store *)((char *)p+nw)
	 && (struct store *)((char *)p+nw) >= p)
		goto found;

The second half asks "did p+nw wrap past the end of memory".  Once the
heap climbs past 0x8000, p+nw is "negative" and p is "positive", so the
guard says the address wrapped and the block is refused - for every
free block above 0x8000, permanently.  malloc then grows the arena by
one granule, searches, is refused by its own guard, and grows again.

Measured over one link, from the instruction trace:

	62 malloc calls
	31 of them grew the arena not at all
	25 grew it once
	 5 grew it twice
	 1 grew it 246 times

246 granules of 129 bytes is 31,734 of the 38,000, spent in a single
call that never returned a block.  The call was asking for fourteen
bytes.  The block it kept refusing was at 0x7ff6 with 162 bytes free in
it; p+nw came to 0x8008.

That is also why it was so hard to find.  Almost every measurement of
malloc says it is healthy, because 61 calls out of 62 are: the same
allocation sequence replayed standalone costs 3,354 bytes for 3,152
asked.  The heap only misbehaves after it crosses a boundary that
depends on how big the program is, so the failure moved every time
anything was rebuilt, and every instrumented build - being bigger -
died at a different place than the clean one.


Why it stayed hidden
--------------------

Nothing else in the tree grows through 0x8000 while holding pointers on
both sides of it.  The compiler passes allocate from permalloc, which
hands out pieces of a chunk and never compares two arena pointers.  The
tools mostly index rather than compare.  malloc is the one program that
must compare arbitrary heap addresses, and the heap is the one thing
that grows across the middle of memory.

It is not an exotic case, though, and that is the point: "p >= q",
"while (p < end)", "is this pointer inside that buffer" are ordinary,
and every one of them is wrong today for a buffer that straddles
0x8000.  On this machine that is a normal place for a buffer to be.


Not fixed here
--------------

Documented first, deliberately.  The fix is in pass1's type lowering -
a pointer-valued expression should go into the AST as unsigned - and it
wants a runtime test alongside it, in tests/run, that compares two
pointers across 0x8000 and fails on the compiler as it stands.  Without
that test this comes back the first time anything touches the lowering,
and it will be just as invisible the second time.
